ing the relation between  permutations and totally symmetric and  antisymmetric states  we need to consider what happens to  observables  let's start with a simple example  corresponding to a two particle system  in this case the permutation operator  p21 exchanges the two particles  consider hermitian operator a1 that acts  on the single particle state space v1  and whose eigenvalue equation is a1  acting on eigenstate ui  equals eigenvalue ai multiplying  eigenstate ui  a1 is hermitian so its eigenstates form  a basis of v1  there is also an operator a2 acting on  v2 with the corresponding eigenvalue  equation  and the eigenstates also form a basis  for v2  now promote a1 to act on the tensor  product state space v  as usual and i will use this simplified  notation emitting the identity operator  and we can similarly promote a2 to act  on v  to understand the interplay between  permutations and general operators we  start by considering the combination p21  a1 p21 dagger we consider its action on  the basis state of v  which we can build as the tensor product  of the eigenstates of a1 and a2  as p21 is hermitian and it is simply the  action of the permutation operator  so we get this expression where the  particles are essentially exchanged  then we get the action of a1 on the  eigenstates from v1  which gives this and finally we act with  the p21 permutation operator to exchange  the particles back  in parallel consider the action of a2 on  the same basis  state a2 acts on the basis ket in v2  and we obtain this expression comparing  these two expressions we see that they  are the same  so that p21 a1 p21 dagger is equal to a2  in a similar way we can show that p21 a2  p21 dagger is equal to a1  so what does this mean the permutation  operator exchanges the action of an  operator from v1 to v2  and vice versa  these two results for the action of the  permutation operator on operators that  act only on v1 or only on v2 generalizes  two operators that act in the full  tensor product state space v for example  consider the operator c12  which is the sum over a1 and b2  remember that what we really mean by  this is the sum of the tensor product of  a1 with the identity in v2  and the tensor product of the identity  in v1 with b2  to see how c12 transforms on the  permutation operator  we start by explicitly writing out c12  in terms of a1 and b2  then we carry out the multiplication to  get this  then we get the action of the  permutation operator on a1 and b2  to get this and finally we can rewrite  it as c21  we can do the same for an operator d12  that is a product of a1 and b2  looking at how it transforms under  permutation we start with this  then we write out d12 we can then insert  p21 dagger  p21 in the middle here and we can do  this as this is the identity operator  because p21 is a unitary operator  then we can group the terms like this  and we get a to b1  that we can finally rewrite as d21  putting these results together we can  write down this general statement about  an observable  o12 acting in v that can be written in  terms of observables that act on v1  and v2 we're now ready to define  symmetric  observables a symmetric observable  is an observable for which o12 is equal  to o21 this definition is in fact  equivalent  to saying that the symmetric observable  is one that commutes with the  permutation operator  to see this consider the action of the  permutation operator on o12  which gives o to one we then insist that  the observable is symmetric  so it is equal to one two  we can then multiply by p to one from  the right on both sides of the equation  and we get this  this term gives simply the identity  because p21 is unitary so we obtain that  p21012  is equal to o12 p21  this implies that an observable that  commutes with the permutation operator  is a symmetric observable  we can generalize these results to  systems of n particles  and we define a totally symmetric  observable under exchange of indices